WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.180
In the gonad of a worm larva, two cells lie side by side.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.720
They are named Z1.ppp and Z4.aaa.

3
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.000
In every worm, one becomes the anchor cell.

4
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.060
The other becomes a ventral uterine precursor.

5
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.620
But which is which varies.

6
00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:27.720
Each cell takes the anchor fate in about half of animals.

7
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:36.040
Kill Z1.ppp with a laser, and Z4.aaa becomes the anchor cell.

8
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.520
Kill Z4.aaa instead, and Z1.ppp does.

9
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.660
Whichever one you kill, the survivor always becomes the anchor cell.

10
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:49.160
So the other fate needs a neighbour.

11
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:58.003
Greenwald's thesis work, with Paul Sternberg and Robert Horvitz, tied the choice to a gene called lin-12.

13
00:00:58.903 --> 00:01:02.203
With no lin-12, both cells become anchor cells.

14
00:01:02.843 --> 00:01:05.503
With overactive lin-12, neither cell does.

15
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:12.677
Now take mosaic worms, in which only one of the two cells lacks lin-12.

16
00:01:13.297 --> 00:01:16.377
That cell always becomes the anchor cell.

17
00:01:17.437 --> 00:01:21.017
What circuit between two equal cells could produce that?
